  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nScattered rain showers continue to push to the east across the\nregion ahead of a warm front over central PA into western NY.\nThis front is driving clouds across the area, which should keep\ntemperatures a few degrees cooler than guidance suggests,\nclimbing to the mid 60s to low 70s. WAA behind the front will \npush warm and moist air into the region overnight, keeping \novernight lows 10-15 degrees warmer than normal, only bottoming\nout in the low to mid 60s. \n\nA cold front will dive into the area from the NW Friday morning\nas a broad trough moves across the Great Lakes and into the\nNortheast. Rain showers will accompany the front, moving from \nWNW to ESE starting in the early morning hours across the\nnorthern Finger Lakes region. These showers will slowly move to\nthe ESE as the front pushes through CNY and into NEPA by the\nlate afternoon hours. Rain is expected to exit the Catskills and\nPoconos area by the evening hours. The Finger Lakes into the\nMohawk Valley currently should see about 0.1-0.25 inches, with\nlower amounts to the east and south. There will be a decent \nhigh temperature gradient associated with the front with low to \nmid 60s by the afternoon north of the Southern Tier and upper \n60s to mid 70s along and south of the Southern Tier. The timing \nof the front will likely mean early overnight temps in the mid \nto upper 60s will be the high temperatures for the day across \nthe Mohawk Valley and N Finger Lakes. High pressure will build \ninto the region behind the cold front, bringing cooler and drier\nweather for the weekend. Highs in the 60s with dewpoints in the\n40s are expected.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHigh pressure will be the main weather driver for the week, but\na pesky trough moving into the area Sunday into Monday morning\nmay kick off some scattered rain showers across eastern portions\nof the area. Guidance remains somewhat uncertain with how the\ntrough will evolve as the northern and southern branches of the\njet stream look to phase together somewhere along the east\ncoast. Current guidance sees the phasing and off the eastern\nseaboard, which would limit rain shower chances across our area.\nIf phasing can occur Sunday night instead of Monday morning, we\ncould get some heavier rain to develop over the area. We will \nmonitor how this trough develops. \n\nAnother strong high pressure system will move into the area\nbehind the departing trough. This ridge will bring the first\nreal taste of fall as Canadian air cascades over the region.\nHighs on Monday will be in the mid to upper 50s for most of CNY\nand low to mid 60s in NEPA. Tuesday will be even colder, with\nhighs in the upper 40s to low 50s across much of the area. Mid\n50s will be possible across the valleys of the Southern Tier\ninto NEPA. Overnight lows will be cold! Monday night will see\nlows in the low to mid 30s with some upper 20s in northern\nOneida county. Tuesday night will be a few degrees colder, with\nmany spots falling to freezing or near freezing. Much of the \ngrowing season in CNY has ended, but if you still have plants \noutside or veggies that need to be harvested, take proper \nactions these two nights. Southerly flow should return \nWednesday, bringing a warming trend through the rest of the week\nwith temps getting back to average by the end of the week.",
